{"id":"8060137257b89e0c473ee1cc84fb4d8942c01dac397320d338fc479ef51b7b76","lamport":1,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-10T12:12:27.929880001Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"## What to build\n\nThe code-review record (create, approve, request changes, revision history with patch-id and change-id, merge, \"all approved\") becomes a lib module in the same way Writeups are. `ti review` and the TUI become thin callers of one interface. Calls to git and the `but` tool sit behind a small adapter so lib tests can use a fake.\n\n## Why\n\nThe review feature (about 1.1k lines) lives in the CLI, and about 10 near-identical helpers are duplicated again inside the TUI. Both write review metadata directly through raw git-meta targets (about 42 direct calls in the TUI). Nothing about the review rules can be tested without driving the binary or a terminal; existing TUI tests only check formatting.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Review rules (approvals, revision change history, all-approved) are covered by lib tests through the test store and a fake VCS adapter\n- [ ] The CLI review commands and the TUI review views call the lib; the duplicated helpers are deleted\n- [ ] No raw review metadata keys are written from the binary crate\n- [ ] A real adapter (git / `but`) and a fake adapter both exist, justifying the seam\n- [ ] Existing review CLI integration tests pass unchanged; `--json` output and schema are unchanged\n- [ ] TUI review behaviour is unchanged (manual check noted in the PR)\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone — can start immediately.\n\n## Notes\n\nNo ADR conflict. Consider tackling in two steps (lib module with the CLI as the first caller, then the TUI) if it does not fit one context window."}}}